  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Unsettled weather expected through midweek as a \nfront remains stalled over the region...temperatures trending \nnoticeably cooler by Wednesday.\n\nShowers and storms developed along the Blue Ridge earlier this \nafternoon and moved southeast into the piedmont this evening,\nwith a few lingering storms closer to the mountains. An area of\nSBCAPE near 2000 J/kg in the vicinity of the southern Blue \nRidge is fueling the lingering storms along and west of \nInterstate 77. A few stronger storms may still be possible, but \nthe environment has been overall pretty marginal with respect \nto severe storm and excessive rainfall potential, and time for \nstorms to intensify is quickly diminishing with the loss of \ndaytime heating and instability. DCAPE of 500-800 J/kg should \nenhance the downdraft winds with any shower activity with \npotential to produce some 25 to 35 mph wind gusts with the \nstronger storms. Not overly concerned with excessive rainfall \nper ongoing drought and transient nature of any activity that \ndevelops through this evening. Once the sun goes down, expect \nstorms to weaken.\n\nAnother 24 hours of heat and humidity is expected, then we \nshould begin to trend much cooler. A front to our north will be \nthe clash zone for a multi-day period of shower activity with \nseveral waves of convectively induced vorticity moving along the\nfront spawning periodic showers and thunderstorms as the front \nworks its way through the area Monday and Tuesday. Passage of \nthe front Monday night will bring a wind shift from the north \nand onset of a cooler air airmass. This cooler airmass will \nthen deepen and become more pronounced Wednesday into Thursday \nas a coastal storm develops along the northern Mid Atlantic \nCoast with model trends developing this into a hybrid \nsubtropical storm off the New England Coast...a feature that \nlooks to influence our weather for multiple days in the form of \nstrong cold air advection...winds blowing from the north through\nFriday. Persistent cloud cover associated with the colder \nairmass will then mute our temperatures with readings 10 to 15 \ndegrees below the seasonal norm and a full 20 to 25 degrees \nbelow current observed values. \n\nDuring this transition, we should experience multiple days of \nshowery weather with potential to receive 1-2\" of rain. This \nis expected to be much needed rainfall, so unless we see the\nactivity become stationary, we do not anticipate issuing any \nflood watch headlines. If there is any flooding it would be \nlocalized and during the time when the front backdoors into the\narea Monday night when storm cell movement along the boundary \ntends to be nearly stationary along Blue Ridge as it wedges into\nthe area from the northeast. \n\nThe bigger story will be the significant cool down with \npotential to remain stuck in the 50s and 60s Wednesday and \nThursday. Cold air advection is expected to persist for multiple\ndays so it may take until the weekend to return to readings near\nnormal.",
